## Chess piece shapes

`src/components/board/piece-sprite.tsx`

**Origin:** original work, drawn for this repository.
**Licence:** MIT, the same as the rest of the application source (`LICENSE`).
**Third-party material:** none. No existing piece set is copied, traced or derived from.

The six shapes — king, queen, rook, bishop, knight, pawn — are drawn as single SVG paths on the
conventional 45x45 grid. Colour is not part of a shape: `<use>` supplies the fill and stroke, so
each shape serves both its white and its black piece.

### Why these are not from an existing set

The ticket asked for a permissively licensed open set. Drawing them instead was a deliberate
departure, for two reasons, and it satisfies the same underlying concerns more completely.

- **Size.** ADR-0003 budgets roughly 2KB for the whole board. Detailed sets — Cburnett and its
  descendants — carry one to three kilobytes of path data *per piece*, so twelve of them would
  exceed the entire board's budget several times over before a single square is drawn. Simple
  shapes are also more legible at the size a branch preview renders them.
- **Licence.** The best-known open set, Cburnett, is multi-licensed and its options include
  GPL-family terms. ADR-0003 already rejected `@lichess-org/chessground` over exactly that risk.
  Original work under the repository's own licence removes the question rather than analysing it.

### Inlining

The shapes are inlined in the bundle at build time and are never fetched. There is no piece URL
anywhere in the application, which is what makes requirement N8 — zero third-party requests at
runtime — true by construction rather than by policy.
